title: Metarecord endpoints
tags: [[Data model]]
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: The resource routes — one metarecord, one field by name, one row by id — plus bulk create, retype and the field catalogue.

The daemon's API has two layers: ''resources'' — one thing at its URL, cheap and
idempotent — and the ''set layer'', `POST …/query/*`, where every body carries a
query. They overlap only on one metarecord (its URL, or a `uuid_in` query). Reading
''several'' metarecords is a query: there is no list or batch-read route.

|!Route |!Does |
|`POST /repos/:repo/metarecords` |creates one: `{"fields": […], "force"?}` → the full metarecord, `id` on each field |
|`POST /repos/:repo/metarecords/bulk` |creates many in one revision (below) |
|`GET /repos/:repo/metarecords/:uuid` |reads one, each field with its row `id` |
|`PUT /repos/:repo/metarecords/:uuid` |replaces its whole field set, keeping the uuid: `{"fields": […], "force"?}` → the metarecord |
|`DELETE /repos/:repo/metarecords/:uuid` |deletes it and its fields → `204`; the repository's root is refused (`400`) |
|`POST /repos/:repo/metarecords/:uuid/fields` |appends one row: `{"name", "value", "force"?}` → the metarecord (unchanged when the value was there) |
|`GET /repos/:repo/metarecords/:uuid/fields/:name` |`{"name", "values": […]}` — empty when the field is unknown |
|`PUT /repos/:repo/metarecords/:uuid/fields/:name` |sets the field: `{"value"}` or `{"values": […]}`, exactly one, `"force"?` → the metarecord |
|`DELETE /repos/:repo/metarecords/:uuid/fields/:name` |unsets it (every row goes; the field becomes unknown) → `204` |
|`GET /repos/:repo/fields/:id` |one row by its id alone: `{"id", "name", "value"}`; `404` when unknown |
|`PATCH /repos/:repo/fields/:id` |changes its name and/or value, keeping the id: `{"name"?, "value"?, "force"?}`; `400` if it would duplicate a sibling |
|`DELETE /repos/:repo/fields/:id` |deletes the row: `{"force"?}` → `204` |
|`POST /repos/:repo/retype` |`{"name", "to"}` → `{"converted", "fallback_count", "fallback_uuids"}` ([[Changing a field's type]]) |
|`GET /repos/:repo/fields[?type=<t>]` |the distinct field names with their type, by name (below) |

The whole-record `PUT` is a literal overwrite: fields not listed go, `mfr_*` and
`mf_*` included — a careless one untracks a file. Read the metarecord first to keep
what should stay. The single-record writes take `?expected_version=`
([[Conditional writes]]). A reserved name needs `force` ([[Reserved fields]]); a
schema violation is a `400` with a `violations` array ([[Delta validation]]).

!! Bulk create

```
{"metarecords": [{"uuid"?: "<32hex>", "fields": […]}, …], "force": false, "skip_existing": false}
→ {"created": N, "skipped": M, "uuids": […]}
```

Each metarecord may bring its uuid, or gets one drawn; `uuids` lists them in the
order given. `force` covers the whole batch. `skip_existing` turns a uuid already
taken into a skip instead of a `409` — a subtree restore meeting a node the watcher
re-tracked meanwhile would otherwise have to query first.

It is ''all or nothing'': one revision, one transaction; the first refusal (a taken
uuid, a reserved field without `force`, a schema violation naming its
`metarecord_uuid`) rolls the batch back. Metarecords are applied ''in the order
given'', and a `tree_ref` whose parent has no row yet is refused ([[Referential integrity of a forest]]), so a subtree comes
parent first. At most 1000 metarecords per call (`400` naming the limit, before any
work); the body is capped at 2 MiB on its own, so fat metarecords page sooner.
Nothing carries a version: a metarecord recreated with its fields gets its old
version back ([[Metarecord version]]); row ids are new.

!! The field catalogue

`GET /fields` answers `[{"name": "category", "type": "tree_ref"}, …]`: each name once
([[One value type per field name]]), `nothing` rows left out, `?type=` keeping one
type. It is read from the store's derived data. The GUI's field pickers use it;
`mf field list` prints it.
